-# Persistent Authentication for Regression Tests
-
-## Overview
-
-The regression test suite uses **persistent authentication** via Playwright's storage state feature. This means:
-
-- ✅ Login happens **once** before all tests
-- ✅ All tests reuse the same authenticated session
-- ✅ Much faster test execution (no login per test)
-- ✅ More reliable (fewer network requests)
-- ✅ Better for CI/CD pipelines
-
-## How It Works
-
-### 1. Global Setup (`global-setup.ts`)
-
-Before any tests run, the global setup script:
-
-```typescript
-// tests/regression/global-setup.ts
-1. Launches a browser
-2. Navigates to test.gazai.io
-3. Performs login with TEST_USER_EMAIL and TEST_USER_PASSWORD
-4. Handles daily token claim dialog
-5. Saves authentication state to .auth/regression-user.json
-6. Closes the browser
-```
-
-This runs **once** at the start of the test suite.
-
-### 2. Test Execution
-
-Each test automatically:
-
-```typescript
-1. Starts with authentication already loaded
-2. Has access to cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage
-3. Is already logged in - no need to call loginUser()
-4. Can immediately start testing features
-```
-
-### 3. Storage State File
-
-The authentication state is saved to:
-
-```
-.auth/regression-user.json
-```
-
-This file contains:
-
-- Cookies
-- localStorage data
-- sessionStorage data
-- Other browser state
-
-**Important**: This file is in `.gitignore` and should never be committed!
-
-## Configuration
-
-### playwright.regression.config.ts
-
-```typescript
-export default defineConfig({
-  // Run global setup once before all tests
-  globalSetup: require.resolve('./tests/regression/global-setup'),
-
-  projects: [
-    {
-      name: 'chromium',
-      use: {
-        // Load saved authentication state
-        storageState: '.auth/regression-user.json'
-      }
-    }
-  ]
-});
-```
-
-## Writing Tests
-
-### Before (with loginUser fixture):
-
-```typescript
-test('My test', async ({ page, loginUser }) => {
-  await loginUser('/some-page'); // Login every time
-  // ... test code
-});
-```
-
-### After (with persistent auth):
-
-```typescript
-test('My test', async ({ page }) => {
-  // Already logged in! Just navigate
-  await navigateToPage(page, '/some-page');
-  // ... test code
-});
-```
-
-## Benefits
-
-### Speed Improvement
-
-- **Before**: Each test logs in (5-10 seconds per test)
-- **After**: Login once (5-10 seconds total)
-- **Result**: For 50 tests, save ~250-500 seconds!
-
-### Reliability
-
-- Fewer network requests = fewer points of failure
-- Less flakiness from login timeouts
-- Consistent authentication state across all tests
-
-### Resource Efficiency
-
-- Less load on test.gazai.io server
-- Faster CI/CD pipeline execution
-- Lower cost for test infrastructure
-
-## Session Lifecycle
-
-### When is the session created?
-
-- At the very start when you run `pnpm test:regression`
-- Before any test files are executed
-
-### How long does the session last?
-
-- For the entire test run
-- All tests in a single run share the same session
-- Next run will create a new session
-
-### When is the session recreated?
-
-- Every time you run `pnpm test:regression`
-- The global setup always runs first
-- Old session is replaced with fresh login
-
-## Handling Session Expiry
-
-If a session expires during test execution:
-
-### Option 1: Automatic Retry
-
-Playwright's built-in retry mechanism will:
-
-1. Detect the test failure
-2. Re-run global setup (fresh login)
-3. Retry the test with new session
-
-### Option 2: Manual Re-authentication
-
-Some tests might need to refresh authentication:
-
-```typescript
-test('Test that might expire session', async ({ page }) => {
-  // Normal test execution
-
-  // If you need to verify login state
-  const isLoggedIn = await page
-    .getByTestId('user-profile-button')
-    .isVisible()
-    .catch(() => false);
-
-  if (!isLoggedIn) {
-    // Session expired - this shouldn't happen often
-    // but you can handle it if needed
-  }
-});
-```
-
-## Debugging
-
-### View the saved authentication state
-
-```bash
-cat .auth/regression-user.json
-```
-
-### Force re-login
-
-```bash
-# Delete the auth file
-rm .auth/regression-user.json
-
-# Run tests - will create fresh login
-pnpm test:regression
-```
-
-### Check if authentication worked
-
-Look for this in test output:
-
-```
-🚀 Global Setup: Logging in to save authentication state...
-   Target: https://test.gazai.io
-   User: e2e@gazai.io
-   Navigating to: https://test.gazai.io/ja-JP
-   🔐 Not logged in, starting login flow...
-   ✅ Login successful
-   ✅ Claimed daily tokens
-   ✅ Authentication state saved to: .auth/regression-user.json
-
-🎉 Global setup complete! All tests will reuse this session.
-```
-
-## CI/CD Integration
-
-### GitHub Actions Example
-
-```yaml
-- name: Run Regression Tests
-  run: pnpm test:regression
-  env:
-    TEST_USER_EMAIL: ${{ secrets.REGRESSION_USER_EMAIL }}
-    TEST_USER_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.REGRESSION_USER_PASSWORD }}
-    BASE_URL: https://test.gazai.io
-```
-
-The global setup will automatically:
-
-1. Use the provided credentials
-2. Login once
-3. Save the session
-4. Run all tests with that session
-
-## Security Considerations
-
-### ✅ Good Practices
-
-- `.auth/` directory is in `.gitignore`
-- Never commit authentication files
-- Use environment variables for credentials
-- Use dedicated test accounts (not real user accounts)
-
-### ⚠️ Important Notes
-
-- Auth files contain session data - treat as secrets
-- Rotate test account passwords periodically
-- Don't share auth files between team members
-- Each developer/CI should create their own sessions
-
-## Comparison: Fixture vs Global Setup
-
-| Aspect          | Fixture (loginUser) | Global Setup      |
-| --------------- | ------------------- | ----------------- |
-| Login frequency | Every test          | Once              |
-| Test speed      | Slower              | Faster            |
-| Complexity      | Simple              | Moderate          |
-| Session sharing | No                  | Yes               |
-| Best for        | Small test suites   | Large test suites |
-| Setup time      | None                | Initial setup     |
-| Maintenance     | Easy                | Easy              |
-
-## Migration Guide
-
-### Step 1: Remove loginUser from tests
-
-```diff
-- test('My test', async ({ page, loginUser }) => {
--   await loginUser('/page');
-+ test('My test', async ({ page }) => {
-+   await navigateToPage(page, '/page');
-```
-
-### Step 2: Verify tests still work
-
-```bash
-pnpm test:regression
-```
-
-### Step 3: Check console output
-
-Look for successful global setup message
-
-## Troubleshooting
-
-### Tests fail with "not logged in" errors
-
-**Solution**: Check global setup logs, ensure credentials are correct
-
-### "Storage state file not found" error
-
-**Solution**: Run tests once to create the file, or check file path
-
-### Tests pass locally but fail in CI
-
-**Solution**: Ensure CI has correct environment variables
-
-### Session seems stale or expired
-
-**Solution**: Delete `.auth/` folder and run tests again
-
-## Files Modified
-
-The persistent authentication system uses these files:
-
-1. **[tests/regression/global-setup.ts](./global-setup.ts)** - Login logic
-2. **[playwright.regression.config.ts](../../playwright.regression.config.ts)** - Configuration
-3. **[.gitignore](../../.gitignore)** - Excludes `.auth/` folder
-4. All test files - Updated to remove `loginUser` fixture
-
-## Summary
-
-✅ **Faster tests** - Login once instead of per-test
-✅ **More reliable** - Fewer network requests
-✅ **Better CI/CD** - Efficient pipeline execution
-✅ **Easy to use** - Tests just work without extra code
-✅ **Secure** - Auth files excluded from git
-
-The persistent authentication is a **best practice** for regression test suites and is now fully implemented!
